>     But then we have the problem that we can't turn the checks off. There was some discussion about adding STAN_DEBUG to
>     have finer grained control of things like this, but I don't know what happened with that. My view is that if someone
>     sets set_cppo("fast") without first verifying that their model is correct, then they deserve to crash. Maybe RStan
>     could set -DDEBUG unless the user calls set_cppo("fast") instead of what we do now which is only set -DDEBUG if the
>     user calls set_cppo("debug").
>
>
> First, we can handle checks using policies. That refactoring is high on my list.

But that's not going to turn the checking off like Ben wants.
The policies only configure what happens when an error is
found.

> Regarding use case, I don't think the users understand the contract that you've described with using set_cppo("fast").
> Unless it's really clear to the user that set_cppo("fast") can crash R and setting it is very deliberate, I think the
> behavior should be to not crash rstan. Is that range check really killing speed? Naively, I would think that other parts
> of Stan would dominate time.

Exactly!

I think we need to let the user run without error checking,
but we need to let them know it's dangerous.

- Bob

