I have no problem either way, provided that in either case the user gets a clear error message so they know what to fix.

> We should think hard about the arg names.  The current
> ones aren't great.  What is int_time?

Total integration time for static HMC (I'm going to be pretty insistent on step size and integration time begin the free parameters for static HMC instead of step size and number of steps).  But in any case the names are easy fixes.

> I don't like the "negative number means infinity" kind
> of things we're doing now (and I did them!).  I'm not
> sure what a good alternative idiom is.  Maybe a different
> command?  That is, I don't like this choice:
>
>   --epsilon=0     // adapt eps
>   --epsilon=.01  // use eps=.01
>
> I would prefer:
>
>   --adapt_epsilon   // adapt
>   --epsilon=.01    // use eps = .01
>   --adapt_epsilon --epsilon=.01 // ILLEGAL!
>
> Thoughts?

Agreed!  I think we can fully factor the various options so that we don't have to overload arguments like this.  For example,

./example sample --iter=2000 --warmup=500 nuts --max_depth=10 --stepsize=0.1 --metric=diag_e adapt --delta=0.65 output --sample_file=samples.csv --diagnostic_file=diagnostic.csv

Then in the parsing we check to determine the final algorithm.  NUTS (check).  Diagonal Euclidean metric (check).  Adaptation (check) ->  adapt_diag_e_nuts.  This is what I had in mind when building the mock-up.

> For defaults, can we have max defaults be the the max
> legal value rather than some value meaning no max?

I think we should be careful about that.  For example, in the case of max_depth for NUTS we don't want misbehaving models to build an infinitely large tree.

> I prefer "./example static_hmc..." to "./example -static_hmc".
>
> I like the double dashes used by git for command property args,
> but am not wedded to it.  I did it originally partly because
> it seemed easier to code.

Trivial change (see above for example).  Again, I have no strong preference other than not building code that people don't like because dashes are in the wrong place.

