> :-)  Makes sense to me.  We'll have to explain this pretty
> clearly.  Static HMC is the alternative to NUTS HMC?

Yessir.

> Are we going to name something to do adaptation during
> warmup?

See the example -- my though is to have adaptation switched with its own flag (adapt=on --delta=0.65).  Figuring out the exact algorithm to use would be a post-argument processing step.

> Are you changing all of these things now or are we
> talking about a subsequent release?

The sampler functionality is there, just waiting on better arguments.  So not until we've redone the arguments.

> I can't even figure out whether sooner or later is better
> for RStan, PyStan, and our poor users who are getting
> a lot thrown at them.

Understandable, but I'm focusing on a schema that enables automated documentation which, along with the verbose error handling, should help users.

> Oh, I just meant defaults that were equal to some
> large value.  For NUTS, we should be careful.  And
> probably also print a warning to the output stream
> when it hits the max depth as a diagnostic.

Sorry -- misread your previous comment as "max floating point value" not "well-chosen maximum".  :-)

> I don't have even a weak preference.  I used the
> double dashes because it was easy to code.

I was going over the code last night and convinced myself that, to really make this robust, we'd want to implement recursive argument handling (hmc is a subargument of sample, but hmc has its own options like algorithm=nuts, which has its own options).  I've sketched out some code that should enable this functionality but such deep hierarchies might not be clear on the command line.  So does anyone have any preference between

(flat) ./example method=sample adapt=on delta=0.65 algorithm=hmc metric=diag_e variant=nuts max_depth=10

verses

(hierarchical flags) ./example method=sample -adapt=on --delta=0.65 -algorithm=hmc --metric=diag_e --variant=nuts ---max_depth=10

?

