No point in getting too fancy with Metropolis.  After all, we have HMC.  The one place Metropolis would be useful, I suppose, is with probability densities whose gradients can't easily be calculated (for example, in a toxiology model where the expected values of data given parameters are spit out from a black-box differential-equation solver).
