I just pushed a big block of commits.  Almost all of it
revolves around matrix and array slicing.   We already
had block().  I added tests for Marcus's sub_row() and sub_col()
and pulled them into their own file.  I also added
vector, row vector and array head() and tail() and segment()
functions.  I cleaned up the print spacing in some of the
error handling.  Everything's tested, documented and added to
function signatures.  Doing this all the "right way" is really
really time consuming --- I don't know how Daniel Lee rips through
this stuff so quickly.

I still haven't produced model files to compile with all the
usages.

I added a validate_std_vector_index in math/matrix, but it should
probably go somewhere else.  I just didn't see a natural place
for it.  I followed the other index error functions in requiring
the adjustment for indexing by 1 on the outside.

Our library is a bit awkward in how it's set up because of
the indexing from 1 in Stan and indexing from 0 in C++.
Our functions typically follow the Stan indexing from 1, but the
underlying std::vector and Matrix objects use indexing from 0.

I also pushed a slight change to the Eigen numerical traits
that would've leaked memory had we continually printed agrad
matrices.  We were returning new agrad::var objects in print.
I realized this from a question on the Eigen list
by Bradley Bell, who's working on adding Eigen to CppAD, another
open-source C++ auto-diff package (I responded on the Eigen
list).

I also applied some patches to the doc and added some
more acknowledgments.

- Bob

From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Mon Apr 29 17:25:51 2013
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Mon, 29 Apr 2013 17:25:51 +0000
Subject: time-limited warmup & covar estimator accumulators
Message-ID: <2sNVxth0A6E.W2QUDkvVMAQJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/2sNVxth0A6E/m/W2QUDkvVMAQJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

For estimating mass matrices for HMC and covar matrices
for Metropolis, we now use batches divided exponentially
like follows:

   51--100, 101--200, 201--400, 401--800

I'm not sure what we do if the user specifies 735
warmup iterations.  Mike had a suggestion to divide
it as 735/2^N.

But what do we do with adaptation for a fixed time?
Then we don't know the magic number 735 ahead of time.

One solution would be to just two accumulators.
There's a current accumulator.  It starts accumulating
sufficient stats at iteration 51.  At iteration 101,
the accumulator is made the old accumulator and a new
one is started.  At iteration 200, the current accumulator
is made the old one and a new one's started and so on.

Then if we stop short, we can just add the old and current
accumulators for the final result.

We could even add the accumulators as we went if the
solver's not too expensive.

We could even use the pair of accumulators to turn off
adaptation early if the mass matrix estimated for the previous
batch is similar to that for the current batch.  Of course,
that only checks that mass matrix estimation has stabilized,
not that we've reached the typical set.

- Bob

